  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1256 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nShower/storms chances northeast of the Pecos River diminish, but \nremain present from heating of elevated terrain over the Marfa \nPlateau, with grids indicating low to medium (25% to 35%) PoPs \nFriday afternoon/evening. PWAT closer to 1.00\", very weak easterly\nflow, and DCAPE below 1000 J/kg suggest most storms will be sub- \nsevere, with gusty winds and brief heavy rainfall and frequent \nlightning the main risks. However, more details in coverage and \npotential limits to severity will be become apparent as we get \ncloser to the end of the week. Highs rise into the 90F-95F range, \n85F-90F higher elevations, and close to the triple digits along \nthe Rio Grande as lows remain similar to past nights. Then, \nshower/storm chances diminish farther this weekend through early \nnext week as mid to upper ridging again develops closer over the \narea. This may allow highs to again rise above the triple digits \nfor the usual warmer spots north of the Rio Grande basin such as \nalong the Pecos River valley and northeast Permian Basin. This far\nout, all we can say for now is that high temperatures will remain\nvery warm with no major cooling trend in sight as we close the \nmonth of August and head into September.",
